Weyland, Beate","birthYear":null,"deathYear":null},{"name":"Sara, Baroni","birthYear":null,"deathYear":null},{"name":"Heidrun, Demo","birthYear":null,"deathYear":null},{"name":"Aline, Lupa","birthYear":null,"deathYear":null},{"name":"Vanessa, Macchia","birthYear":null,"deathYear":null},{"name":"Simone, Seitz","birthYear":null,"deathYear":null}],"subjects":[],"bookshelves":["Category: Society and Social Sciences"],"formats":[{"url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/118357/1/9788860462084.pdf","formatType":"pdf"},{"url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/118357/2/9788860462084.pdf.txt","formatType":"txt"}],"lastModified":"2026-10-20","summary":"Inclusive education is a global challenge and a key strategy for strengthening social justice and democratic structures. This idea guided the ninth bilingual conference in the series ‘Didattica e Inclusione Scolastica – Inclusion in Education’. It focused on the question of education and upbringing geared towards inclusion, sustainability and justice in the context of current crises and contrasts. Building on this foundation, the 31 contributions in this book bring together theoretical frameworks, the latest research findings and pedagogical-didactic concepts for the development of high-quality inclusive education. As such, the volume – which is intended to appeal to both academics and practitioners alike – offers valuable insight into current issues and debates within the field. Translated with DeepL.com (free version)","coverUrl":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/118357/3/9788860462084.pdf.jpg"}